    WEEK 04 / COGNITIVE DISTORTIONS
  </p>

  <h3>
    BREAK UP THE BFFLs:<br>
    THE BRAIN AND COGNITIVE DISTORTIONS
  </h3>

  <div class="iop-essay-body">

    <p>
      <em>Hell, nobody will even notice I'm gone</em>,
      I wrote in my jail journal. It was winter of
      2024, and I had only been incarcerated for a
      month or two. I wasn't receiving mail or many
      text messages, and I was convinced that the
      world outside had already forgotten me. My
      friends, family, and neighbors were all living
      their lives and had erased me from their
      memories. Was this really the case though,
      or was my brain playing tricks on me?
    </p>

    <p>
      I was experiencing automatic thoughts—ones
      that happen so quickly that I failed to
      notice them yet they were affecting my mood.
      They weren't an accurate depiction of reality,
      and their best friends cognitive distortions
      were convincing me that my beliefs about the
      world and myself were facts. Cognitive
      distortions are irrational thoughts that
      shape how people see the world, how they feel,
      and how they act. I wasn't analyzing my
      internal narrative, which would've helped me
      recognize the connections between feelings,
      thoughts, and behaviors. And although
      managing stress in jail is like trying to
      shove your foot into the wrong shoe size,
      it can help develop greater awareness over
      what the brain is saying and it's possible
      to have a better ability to control responses.
      I wish I knew this back then, but I was
      reeling and was just trying to survive.
    </p>

    <p>
      Cognitive distortions and the brain are
      tighter than Cheech and Chong. They are
      used very frequently. This is a
      comprehensive list:
    </p>

    <!-- COGNITIVE DISTORTIONS -->
    <ul class="iop-essay-list">

      <li>
        <strong>Absolute Thinking:</strong>
        Using extreme words like
        <em>always, never, everyone,</em>
        and <em>nobody</em>.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>All-or-Nothing Thinking:</strong>
        Seeing things in extremes,
        with no middle ground.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Blaming:</strong>
        Placing excessive responsibility
        for your problems or feelings
        on others.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Catastrophizing:</strong>
        Assuming the worst possible
        outcome will happen.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Discounting the Positive:</strong>
        Dismissing achievements
        or positive experiences.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Emotional Reasoning:</strong>
        Believing something is true
        simply because it feels true.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Fortune Telling:</strong>
        Predicting negative outcomes
        without sufficient evidence.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Jumping to Conclusions:</strong>
        Making assumptions without
        enough information.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Labeling:</strong>
        Defining yourself or others
        by a single mistake
        or characteristic.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Magnification:</strong>
        Exaggerating the importance
        of mistakes, flaws,
        or problems.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Mental Filtering:</strong>
        Focusing on negative details
        while ignoring positive ones.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Mind Reading:</strong>
        Assuming you know what
        others are thinking.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Minimization:</strong>
        Downplaying accomplishments,
        strengths, or problems.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Overgeneralization:</strong>
        Assuming one experience
        represents a recurring pattern.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Personalization:</strong>
        Taking responsibility for
        things outside your control.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Perfectionism:</strong>
        Setting unrealistic standards
        and treating anything less
        than perfection as failure.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Should/Must Statements:</strong>
        Imposing rigid expectations
        on yourself or others.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Unfair Comparisons:</strong>
        Comparing your weaknesses
        to other people's strengths.
      </li>

    </ul>


    <p>
      Thoughts, emotions, and behaviors all
      influence each other in what is known
      as the cognitive triangle, as shown below.
    </p>

   
      <%= image_tag "cognitive_triangle.jpg",
          alt: "Cognitive triangle showing thoughts, emotions, and behaviors",
          class: "iop-essay-image",
          loading: "lazy" %>
   


    <p>
      It demonstrates the relationship between
      all three, and it represents the relationship
      of how the patterns can either reinforce
      negative experiences or encourage healthier
      responses. Cognitive distortions fit into
      this because they represent patterns of
      thinking that can cause us to interpret
      situations inaccurately. When a distorted
      or untrue thought passes through without
      question, it influences how we act, feel,
      and behave. Our behavior can reinforce
      the original thought, and this creates
      a cycle that becomes is difficult to
      recognize, interrupt, or break.
    </p>

    <p>
      The key is to understand the relationship
      between the cognitive triangle and cognitive
      distortions. It's not possible to always
      control our automatic thoughts or our initial
      thoughts, but it's possible to learn to
      question our assumptions and choose an
      appropriate response. Changing one part of
      the triangle can change and influence the
      others, making it possible to break patterns
      instead of repeating habits and patterns.
    </p>

    <p>
      Other elements can influence cognitive
      distortions. Stressors play a big role
      in their appearances. A stressor is an
      internal or external event that activates
      an automatic stress reaction in the body,
      and a stress reaction is a neurological
      or physiological reaction to encountering
      a perceived threat. When this occurs,
      it's recommended to complete the stress
      cycle by doing:
    </p>

    <!-- STRESS CYCLE -->
    <ul class="iop-essay-list">

      <li>Physical activity</li>
      <li>Breathing</li>
      <li>Positive social interaction</li>
      <li>Laughter</li>
      <li>Affection</li>
      <li>Creative self-expression</li>
      <li>Cry</li>

    </ul>


    <p>
      Let's return to my wild statement I made
      in jail: "Hell, nobody will even notice
      I'm gone." What was really going on here?
    </p>

